From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Yi Xie <imxieyi@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Tue, 17 Nov 2026 21:17:03 +0900
Subject: [PATCH] um: name the frame a kernel write into another process
 changed

A ptrace, /proc/pid/mem or process_vm_writev() store reaches another
process's memory through the kernel's own alias of the frame, and the
host was told only the user address written. The target may map another
frame at that address before the store lands: process_vm_writev() pins
the pages and drops the target's mmap lock before it copies from the
caller's buffer, which can wait on a userfaultfd handler. The host then
published at the frame mapped by now, and the holders of the frame
actually written kept their translated code.

The kernel holds the page it writes, so it names it: phys_mapping() of
the page gives the (fd, offset) with which a mapping of that frame is
installed. darwin_uml_vm_invalidate_code_frame() takes the generation,
a user range within one page and that name, and the host publishes at
the frame's holders whatever the generation maps at the address now. A
frame Linux cannot name (a negative fd) is told to every generation, as
a page the host has no entry for is.

__access_remote_vm() holds the page it wrote, and process_vm_writev()
passes the pages it pinned to a new flush_icache_remote_user_pages()
hook, one call per page of the batch. An AArch32 cacheflush() writes
nothing and is still told by address, through
flush_icache_remote_user_range(), which only a kernel that runs AArch32
tasks builds, so the x86_64 slice no longer imports that entry. The
bridge ABI goes to 27, which also declares
darwin_uml_vm_invalidate_code_frames() for a kernel write into a
page-cache frame (the x86_64 slice's flush_dcache_folio() calls it).

Co-Authored-By: Claude Sonnet 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
